          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Tartaroō denotes God’s casting of sinful angels into gloomy confinement, where they are held pending final judgment; it is generally distinguished from the realm of the human dead and from final Gehenna punishment.\",\"book_summary\":\"Tartaroō means casting sinful angels into Tartarus—chains of darkness while awaiting judgment—and explicitly excludes both the grave of the human dead and Gehenna’s final verdict.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Temporary dark confinement of sinful angels awaiting judgment.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Temporary confinement of sinful angels in chains of darkness awaiting judgment.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"None material. The book’s explicit exclusion of the human grave and Gehenna states distinctions already present in the consensus’s primary reading.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book conflicts only with listed alternative or popular conflations of Tartarus with Hades or Gehenna, not with the consensus core.\",\"rationale\":\"After reducing both readings to their minimum identification, both define Tartarus as the interim punitive confinement of sinning angels pending judgment. The book’s negative clarifications sharpen wording but do not change the consensus referent, extension, or conditions.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Although 2 Peter 2:4 and Jude 6 directly support angelic confinement pending judgment, they do not explicitly name Hades or Gehenna; the book’s exclusion of those categories partly depends on contextual and terminological inference. This does not create a consensus disagreement.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
